@host = http://localhost:5000
@subject = dev-user
@tenant = dev
@roles = system_admin

### Readiness — never calls the model, so it is the safe poll
GET {{host}}/alive

### The module is actually loaded. A module that never loads still compiles.
GET {{host}}/api/platform/modules
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### The Requisitions tab's data endpoint
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/requisitions
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### The same endpoint as compliance — MUST be 403. That tier reads impact reports, never candidates.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/requisitions
X-Dev-Subject: it-compliance
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: hiring-compliance

### The truth about permissions: both registration sites, and the approval flags
GET {{host}}/api/admin/security/catalog
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### A read turn. Expect list_requisitions and NO approval_required.
POST {{host}}/api/agui/hiring
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

{ "messages": [ { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Which requisitions still need a rubric?" } ] }

### A write turn. Expect propose_rubric, CUSTOM(approval_required), and a reply that does NOT
### claim the rubric is approved or usable for screening.
POST {{host}}/api/agui/hiring
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

{ "messages": [ { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Propose a rubric for REQ-142 from its job description" } ] }

### The same write turn as a sourcer. The tool must never even be offered — RBAC runs before the
### model request is built, so expect NO propose_rubric in the tool calls.
POST {{host}}/api/agui/hiring
Content-Type: application/json
X-Dev-Subject: it-sourcer
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: hiring-sourcer

{ "messages": [ { "id": "m1", "role": "user", "content": "Propose a rubric for REQ-142 from its job description" } ] }

### Pending approvals — where a parked write waits for a human
GET {{host}}/api/chat/approvals
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Tool-call audit — in this product the audit trail is a deliverable, not just a safety net
GET {{host}}/api/admin/audit/tool-calls
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Per-tenant token spend
GET {{host}}/api/admin/usage?days=30
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}


### The Candidate tab's data endpoint — applicant, live screening, and the CV already split into
### highlight segments. The segmenting is server-side on purpose (CvHighlighting): the span
### arithmetic is the product's central guarantee and belongs where the test ladder can assert it.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/candidates/APP-1001
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Same endpoint as compliance — MUST be 403. It returns a candidate CV, the most personal payload
### in the product, and that tier reads impact reports only.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/candidates/APP-1001
X-Dev-Subject: it-compliance
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: hiring-compliance

### Unknown reference — MUST be 404, not an empty shell.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/candidates/APP-NOPE
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### The Pipeline board's data endpoint — every stage as a column, in stage order, with the
### candidates standing in each. READ ONLY: there is deliberately no write counterpart. Dragging a
### card proposes a move; advancing and rejecting go through the approval-gated tools.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/pipeline?requisition=REQ-142
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### No requisition named — opens on the first by reference, deterministically. A board has to open
### on something, and "whichever the database returned" is not that.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/pipeline
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### Same endpoint as compliance — MUST be 403. It lists candidates by name, and that tier is
### deliberately denied every candidate read so the person auditing decisions cannot influence them.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/pipeline?requisition=REQ-142
X-Dev-Subject: it-compliance
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: hiring-compliance

### Unknown requisition — MUST be 404, not an empty board.
GET {{host}}/api/hiring/pipeline?requisition=REQ-NOPE
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: {{roles}}

### The move that MUST NOT exist — MUST be 404/405. A stage-writing route here would let
### hiring-recruiter (holds ManageApprovals, deliberately NOT advance_candidates) advance a
### candidate, because ApprovalExecutor re-invokes an approved tool without re-checking its
### permission. Guarded by PipelineTests.The_board_offers_no_route_that_moves_a_candidate_between_stages.
PUT {{host}}/api/hiring/pipeline/APP-1001
X-Dev-Subject: {{subject}}
X-Dev-Tenant: {{tenant}}
X-Dev-Roles: hiring-recruiter
Content-Type: application/json

{ "stage": "Interview" }
